const e={note:"Corrected batch. Every native string re-verified character by character with python3 unicodedata; one sign error found and fixed (ŠU.ÍL.LÁ was written with LA, U+121B7, where lá requires the LAL sign, U+121F2). Where a writing could not be assembled from confirmed signs — ÉN, KA.LUḪ.Ù.DA, dDÌM.ME — the transliteration stands in the native field and the gloss says so and says why. Factual corrections concentrate on the Assur exorcists' family, the Alamdimmû tablet count, the Bārûtu chapter structure, and the Assurbanipal dream episode. Three interpretative claims have been downgraded to match the evidence: the substitute-king ritual, the akītu recitation of Enūma Eliš as an annual re-enactment of creation, and the seasonal reading of Dumuzi's alternation.",themes:[{slug:"exorcistic-series",title:"The Exorcistic Series",native:"𒇽𒈦𒈦",translit:"LÚ.MAŠ.MAŠ — āšipu; the craft, āšipūtu",summary:"The craft of the exorcist, its curriculum set out on a single tablet, and the great ritual series that curriculum names.",paragraphs:["The clearest window on to any ancient esoteric profession is a tablet from Assur, catalogued as KAR 44 and known to scholarship as the Exorcist's Manual or Vademecum. It belongs to the library of a family of exorcists in the inner city — the archive designated N4, whose scribes traced their descent from Bāba-šuma-ibni and whose best-attested members are Kiṣir-Aššur and his nephew Kiṣir-Nabû — and further manuscripts are known from Nineveh and from Seleucid Uruk, which tells us the list was not one man's private note but a professional standard. It gives about a hundred entries, each a series title or an incipit, and totals them as the titles of āšipūtu. The first section runs through the canonical ritual series and the rites of daily practice; a second section opens on to a further, denser body of lore and is placed under the authority of Esagil-kīn-apli, the eleventh-century Borsippan scholar who had put the diagnostic and physiognomic corpora in order — though whether he composed the whole document, only its second half, or neither, remains argued over. It is at once a syllabus, a stock-list and a claim: this, and not that, is what an exorcist knows.","At the head of the ritual corpus stands Maqlû, 'Burning', eight tablets of incantations with a ninth, the ritual tablet, giving the actions that accompany them. Nearly a hundred incantations were recited in a single sequence: on Abusch's reconstruction, now generally accepted, one long ceremony beginning in the evening, addressed first to the gods of the night — the stars, who alone are awake and can act as judges when the sun has gone down — and carried through to sunrise. The ritual tablet sets it on the night of the twenty-eighth of Abu, the fifth month, which the calendar associated with the dead. Its subject is kišpū, witchcraft: the sufferer holds that a warlock or, far more often, a witch has bound him, and the ceremony answers by making her in effigy from bitumen, tallow, wax, clay, dough and wood, and destroying her figures in the fire of Girra, the fire-god, under the eye of Nusku. The logic is legal rather than merely violent: the sufferer stood before the night gods as a plaintiff, the case was tried, the sentence executed in the flame, and the morning rites washed the residue of the business off him as the sun came up.","Beside it stands Šurpu, 'Incineration', nine tablets, and the pairing is instructive because the two look superficially alike and are opposite in diagnosis. Maqlû assumes a human enemy; Šurpu assumes none. Its patient is bound by a māmītu — an oath, a curse, a contagion of broken taboo — and does not know which. The remedy is therefore an exhaustive recitation of everything he might have done: the celebrated second tablet catalogues offences from shifting a boundary stone to refusing the beggar, from insulting a parent to promising and not giving, from touching an unclean thing to estranging friend from friend, and it was recited over him not as accusation but as a net cast wide enough that the true fault lay somewhere inside it. Meanwhile objects were unwrapped, unpeeled and unravelled over him and burnt — an onion, a date, a tuft of wool, a length of matting — so that the binding was loosed strand by strand as the fire took them.","The rest of the curriculum is as carefully differentiated. Bīt rimki, the 'bath house', is a royal rite: the king passed through a sequence of reed-built houses, was washed at each and prayed over with hymns to Šamaš, and it was employed above all to carry off the evil an eclipse had aimed at his person. Bīt mēseri, the 'house of enclosure', works the other way, drawing a boundary rather than crossing one: the patient was shut inside a circle of flour, and figurines of the antediluvian sages set at the head of his bed, its third tablet preserving the list of the seven apkallū by name. Udug-ḫul, sixteen tablets in Sumerian with Akkadian rendered line by line beneath, is the general armoury against the evil utukku and his kind. Characteristically the tablet gives the incantation and then, under the rubric 'its ritual', the actions: the censer, the torch, the holy-water vessel, the figurine, the flour, the number of recitations prescribed. Word and act were transmitted together, and neither was thought to work alone."],terms:[{native:"𒇽𒈦𒈦",translit:"lúMAŠ.MAŠ — āšipu",gloss:"The exorcist, written with the doubled MAŠ sign under the LÚ 'man' determinative; also read mašmaššu. Not a sorcerer but a licensed professional of the temple and palace, physician of the invisible causes of illness."},{native:"āšipūtu",translit:"āšipūtu",gloss:"Given here in transliteration: the abstract noun 'the exorcist's craft' is normally written with the MAŠ.MAŠ logogram plus a phonetic complement, and syllabic spellings differ from manuscript to manuscript."},{native:"𒉆𒂟",translit:"NAM.ÉRIM — māmītu",gloss:"Oath, curse, the binding contracted by broken taboo; the condition Šurpu exists to loose. The signs are NAM and ÉRIM, which the Unicode repertoire names ERIN2."},{native:"maqlû",translit:"maqlû",gloss:"'Burning'. Given in transliteration: the title is written syllabically in the manuscripts and has no fixed logogram."},{native:"𒂍",translit:"É — bītu",gloss:"'House'. The element that names the enclosing rituals — bīt rimki, the bath house; bīt mēseri, the house of enclosure — each a temporary architecture of reed and flour raised around one body."},{native:"nēmequ",translit:"nēmequ",gloss:"'Wisdom', the term used of the deeper lore listed in the second section of the Exorcist's Manual. Given in transliteration."}],texts:[{title:"The Exorcist's Manual (KAR 44)",note:"Assur, from the exorcists' house N4, with further manuscripts from Nineveh and Seleucid Uruk; some hundred entries in two sections, the second placed under the authority of Esagil-kīn-apli. Edited by C. Jean (SAAS 17, 2006) and M. J. Geller (2018)."},{title:"Maqlû, 'Burning'",note:"Eight tablets of incantations plus a ritual tablet; nearly a hundred incantations performed through a single night into sunrise, against witchcraft. Critical edition by T. Abusch (2015–16)."},{title:"Šurpu, 'Incineration'",note:"Nine tablets, for a man bound by an unknown curse; Tablet II is the long catalogue of possible offences. Edited by E. Reiner (AfO Beiheft 11, 1958)."},{title:"Bīt rimki, 'The Bath House'",note:"A royal purification through a series of reed houses with hymns to Šamaš, employed after eclipse portents. Studied by J. Læssøe (1955), with the third house edited by R. Borger (1967)."},{title:"Bīt mēseri, 'The House of Enclosure'",note:"The patient enclosed in a circle of flour and guarded by figurines of the sages; the third tablet preserves the names of the seven apkallū (E. Reiner, 1961)."},{title:"Udug-ḫul (Utukkū lemnūtu), 'Evil Utukku Demons'",note:"Sixteen tablets, Sumerian with interlinear Akkadian, the general series against demonic assault. Edited by M. J. Geller (BAM 8, 2016)."}]},{slug:"omen-literature",title:"Omen Literature",native:"𒇽𒄬",translit:"LÚ.ḪAL — bārû; the craft, bārûtu",summary:"The conditional sentence as a scientific instrument, and the enormous series that collected the world's legible surface.",paragraphs:["Every omen in the corpus has the same shape: a protasis introduced by šumma, 'if', and an apodosis stating what follows. On the tablet the protasis is commonly marked by a single vertical wedge, DIŠ, standing at the head of the line like a bullet, so that a page of omens reads as a column of wedges each opening a case. The relation between the two halves is not causal — the fox running into the city does not make the king die — but semiotic. The gods were held to write, and the world's surface is what they write upon: the sky is called the writing of the firmament, the sheep's liver a tablet on which the sun-god sets his answer, a monstrous birth a sentence in a script the scholar has been trained to read. This is why the discipline could be extended by permutation. Scribes systematically generated the cases their schema required even when observation could not supply them — eclipses on days when an eclipse is astronomically impossible sit calmly in the series — because the aim was a complete grammar of signs, not a record of events.","The terrestrial series is Šumma ālu ina mēlê šakin, 'If a city is set on a height', which runs to something like a hundred and twenty tablets and is the nearest thing antiquity produced to a total inventory of ordinary life read as portent. It works outward from the settlement: cities and their sites, houses and their bricks and doorways, fire in the hearth, then snakes, scorpions, ants, lizards, birds, dogs that urinate on a man, oxen and pigs, and eventually the whole conduct of a household including its sexual life. Nothing is too small. A gecko falling on a man is an event with consequences, and the series' patient accumulation of such cases is the reason it remains one of the richest sources we have for how Mesopotamians actually lived.","Two further series read the body. Šumma izbu, twenty-four tablets, treats the anomalous birth — the izbu, malformed lamb or child — and derives from it a public rather than a private future, characteristically the fate of the land, the army or the king; Old Babylonian forerunners show the tradition running back half a millennium before the canonical edition. Alamdimmû, 'form', twelve tablets in the series proper, reads the normal body instead: the shape of the skull, the set of the eyes, moles, gait, the timbre of a voice, taken as a legible index of a man's destiny. Attached to it in the catalogues are the subsidiary sub-series — Nigdimdimmû and Kataduggû on bearing and utterance, and a section on the marks of women. Both corpora were given their fixed form by the same editor, Esagil-kīn-apli, scholar to Adad-apla-iddina of Babylon in the eleventh century, who is styled a descendant of Asalluḫi-mansum, the sage of Hammurabi's court.","Extispicy — bārûtu, the craft of the bārû, the LÚ.ḪAL — stood somewhat apart, because its sign was solicited rather than observed. The diviner put a question, the sun-god was asked to place a firm answer in the flesh of a lamb, and the liver and lungs were then read part by part: the manzāzu or 'station', the padānu or 'path', the bāb ekalli or 'gate of the palace', the ubānu or 'finger', the martu or gall bladder, the tīrānu or coils of the intestines. The canonical corpus was arranged in ten chapters, each running to several tablets and named for the feature it treats. The Nineveh queries preserve the procedure in its royal form, with the question read out and the clean and unclean features tallied afterwards. And when a sign of any kind came out badly, the system had a remedy of its own: the namburbû, 'its undoing', a rite that unbound the announced future — the object of the omen floated away downriver, the man washed and re-presented to his god — of which Stefan Maul assembled well over a hundred. The sign was information, not sentence; that distinction is the whole moral architecture of Mesopotamian divination."],terms:[{native:"𒁹",translit:"DIŠ — šumma",gloss:"The single vertical wedge that heads an omen line and stands for 'if', marking the protasis; the visual signature of the entire omen literature."},{native:"𒌷",translit:"URU — ālu",gloss:"'City'. The incipit of the terrestrial series opens šumma ālu, 'if a city', and the series is cited by that second word."},{native:"𒇽𒄬",translit:"lúḪAL — bārû",gloss:"The diviner or haruspex, 'the one who inspects'; his craft is bārûtu, the reading of the exta of a sacrificial lamb."},{native:"𒉆𒁔𒁉",translit:"NAM.BÚR.BI — namburbû",gloss:"'Its undoing': the counter-ritual that loosens an announced evil after a portent has been seen. Signs NAM, BÚR (Unicode BUR2) and BI."},{native:"izbu",translit:"izbu",gloss:"The malformed newborn, animal or human, whose anomalies the twenty-four tablets of Šumma izbu read as portents of public fate. Given in transliteration."},{native:"alamdimmû",translit:"alamdimmû",gloss:"'Form, figure' — the physiognomic series, which reads a man's destiny off the proportions and marks of his ordinary body. Given in transliteration."},{native:"šiṭir šamê",translit:"šiṭir šamê / šiṭirti burūmê",gloss:"'The writing of heaven', 'the writing of the firmament': the standing metaphor by which the stars are a text the gods have inscribed. Given in transliteration."}],texts:[{title:"Šumma ālu ina mēlê šakin",note:"'If a city is set on a height' — the terrestrial omen series, some 120 tablets, covering settlements, houses, fire, animals and the conduct of a household. Edition in progress by S. Freedman (1998, 2006, 2017)."},{title:"Šumma izbu",note:"Twenty-four tablets on anomalous births, with Old Babylonian forerunners. Edited by E. Leichty (TCS 4, 1970) and N. De Zorzi (2014)."},{title:"Alamdimmû",note:"The physiognomic series, twelve tablets, with the subsidiary sub-series Nigdimdimmû, Kataduggû and a section on the marks of women; serialised by Esagil-kīn-apli. Edited by B. Böck (AfO Beiheft 27, 2000)."},{title:"Bārûtu",note:"The canonical extispicy corpus in ten chapters of several tablets each, named for features of the exta — the station, the path, the gate of the palace, the finger, the gall bladder, the coils."},{title:"Queries to the Sungod (SAA 4)",note:"Nineveh tablets recording royal questions put to Šamaš through extispicy, with the features tallied and the question restated. Edited by I. Starr (1990)."},{title:"The namburbi corpus",note:"Well over a hundred rituals for undoing an announced evil, assembled and edited by S. Maul, Zukunftsbewältigung (1994)."}]},{slug:"celestial-divination",title:"Celestial Divination",native:"𒇽𒁾𒊬",translit:"LÚ.DUB.SAR — ṭupšarru; the craft, ṭupšarrūtu",summary:"Seventy tablets of sky-omens, a network of watchers reporting to the Assyrian court, and the disputed rite by which a king was said to be removed from the path of an eclipse.",paragraphs:["The celestial series is Enūma Anu Enlil, 'When Anu and Enlil', about seventy tablets arranged by the body observed: first the moon and its horns, halos, first visibility and eclipses, then the sun, then the weather-signs of Adad — thunder, cloud, earthquake — then the planets and fixed stars under the sign of Ištar. Its sixty-third tablet is the celebrated Venus Tablet of Ammiṣaduqa, transmitting observations of the planet's first and last visibilities from the reign of the tenth king of Hammurabi's dynasty into the libraries of Nineveh a thousand years later, and standing at the centre of every modern argument about early Mesopotamian chronology — which is why it is better not to date it by a century here, since the century is precisely what is at issue. The men who owned this series were styled not āšipū but ṭupšarrū, the 'scribes' of Enūma Anu Enlil; the titles are kept distinct in the correspondence, though in practice a single scholar might hold more than one competence.","What makes this discipline unique among ancient sciences is that we possess its working papers. Assyrian kings maintained a maṣṣartu, a 'watch', with observers stationed in Nineveh, Assur, Arbela, Babylon, Nippur, Uruk and Borsippa, and their reports survive by the hundreds: a short note of what was seen, then the relevant omen quoted verbatim from the series, then, if the scholar dared, an interpretation. Alongside them run the letters — Issar-šumu-ēreš the chief scribe, Adad-šumu-uṣur the chief exorcist, Balasî who tutored the crown prince, Akkullānu, Bēl-ušēzib, and Mar-Issar reporting from Babylonia. They quote, they hedge, they contradict one another, they complain about rivals and about arrears of pay, and they occasionally tell the king plainly that the sign he is afraid of does not apply to him. It is the most intimate record of a scholarly profession to survive from the ancient world.","MUL.APIN is the compendium that made such work possible: two tablets gathering the astronomical framework rather than the omens, with a star catalogue distributing some seventy stars and constellations among the three 'paths' of Enlil, Anu and Ea — northern, central and southern bands of the sky — followed by dates of heliacal risings, the culminating ziqpu stars used to tell the hours, simultaneous risings and settings, the ideal 360-day year with its schemes for intercalation, shadow-length tables and a water-clock scheme. Its earliest dated manuscript is of 687 BC, though the material it systematises is older. From this substructure the later Babylonian achievement grows: the Astronomical Diaries, the earliest preserved from 652 BC and the latest from the first century, in which the night's phenomena, the river level, the market prices and the political news were recorded together, month upon month, across some six hundred years.","When an eclipse announced death for the king, the scholars are said to have had a procedure, and it must be described with care, because almost everything we know of it comes from a single dossier of letters written to Esarhaddon. A šar pūḫi, a substitute king, was installed on the throne with a woman as his queen; the true king withdrew, was addressed as 'the farmer', and observed strict purity while the substitute absorbed the portended evil into his own person. One letter speaks of a term of a hundred days. Mar-Issar reports to Esarhaddon the death of one Damqi, son of the prelate of Akkad, who went to his fate in this office and was buried with honours. That the substitute was in every case put to death, that the rite was regularly performed outside Esarhaddon's reign, and that the sequence ran as Parpola reconstructed it, are inferences from a handful of witnesses rather than things the sources state. The Chronicle of Early Kings — a first-millennium composition looking back nearly a thousand years — remembers an earlier case that went wrong: Erra-imittī of Isin set his gardener Enlil-bāni on the throne as substitute, then died himself swallowing hot broth, and the gardener stayed king. Later, in Persian and Hellenistic Babylon, the same craft produced something new: the horoscope, the earliest dated to 410 BC, in which the positions of the planets at a child's birth are set down for that child alone, and the discipline of kings becomes at last the property of private persons."],terms:[{native:"𒇽𒁾𒊬",translit:"lúDUB.SAR — ṭupšarru",gloss:"'Scribe', but in this context specifically the ṭupšarru Enūma Anu Enlil, the celestial diviner; his craft is ṭupšarrūtu."},{native:"𒀯𒀳",translit:"MUL.APIN",gloss:"'The Plough Star', the incipit and name of the astronomical compendium. MUL, 'star', is written with the three-fold AN sign; APIN is the plough."},{native:"𒀭𒈪",translit:"AN.MI — attalû",gloss:"Eclipse, written with the signs AN ('sky') and MI ('dark'); the single most consequential phenomenon in the corpus, because its evil was held to fall on the king."},{native:"šar pūḫi",translit:"šar pūḫi",gloss:"'Substitute king', the man said to have been enthroned to receive an eclipse's evil in the king's place. Given in transliteration; the practice is attested chiefly in Esarhaddon's scholarly correspondence."},{native:"ikkaru",translit:"ikkaru",gloss:"'The farmer' — the title by which letters address the real king while a substitute occupied the throne. Given in transliteration."},{native:"maṣṣartu",translit:"maṣṣartu",gloss:"'The watch': the standing observation kept by scholars in the Assyrian and Babylonian cities, and the reports it generated. Given in transliteration."}],texts:[{title:"Enūma Anu Enlil",note:"The celestial omen series, some 70 tablets, ordered moon, sun, weather, stars and planets; Tablet 63 is the Venus Tablet of Ammiṣaduqa."},{title:"Astrological Reports to Assyrian Kings (SAA 8)",note:"Some 570 short reports from the observers of the royal watch, each quoting the relevant omen from Enūma Anu Enlil. Edited by H. Hunger (1992)."},{title:"Letters from Assyrian and Babylonian Scholars (SAA 10)",note:"The working correspondence of Esarhaddon's and Assurbanipal's scholars, including Mar-Issar's letters on the substitute king. Edited by S. Parpola (1993)."},{title:"MUL.APIN",note:"Two tablets: star catalogue in the three paths of Enlil, Anu and Ea, risings, ziqpu stars, intercalation and shadow schemes. Edited by H. Hunger and D. Pingree (1989); new edition, Hunger and Steele (2018)."},{title:"The Astronomical Diaries",note:"Nightly records from Babylon, the earliest preserved from 652 BC, combining celestial phenomena with river levels, commodity prices and political events. Edited by A. Sachs and H. Hunger."},{title:"Babylonian horoscopes",note:"Thirty-two texts, the earliest of 410 BC, giving planetary positions at a birth. Edited by F. Rochberg (1998)."}]},{slug:"dream-interpretation",title:"Dream Interpretation",native:"𒈧𒈪",translit:"MÁŠ.GE₆ — šuttu",summary:"The Dream Book and its specialists, and the rites by which a bad dream was cancelled before it could take effect.",paragraphs:["The dream in Mesopotamia is a message, and like every message it has a sender, a carrier and a recipient who may or may not be competent to read it. The carrier is Zaqīqu — also vocalised Ziqīqu — a word that means breath, spirit, phantom, and is at the same time the name of the god of dreams; the compendium that gathers the material is accordingly called Iškar Zaqīqu, 'the Series of Zaqīqu', and was published by A. Leo Oppenheim in 1956 as the Assyrian Dream Book. Its manuscripts come chiefly from Assurbanipal's libraries at Nineveh. The series is reckoned at eleven tablets, but the reconstruction is provisional and Oppenheim lettered several sections rather than numbering them, because their place in the sequence could not be fixed. The entries are omens in the standard conditional form: if a man in his dream does or sees such a thing, then such a thing follows. He flies; he eats his own flesh; he is given a seal, or a dead man's clothing; he descends to the netherworld and is addressed there by name. The apodoses are terse and often reverse the surface sense of the image, which is the mark of a technical hermeneutics rather than a symbolic intuition.","Dreams were not read by their dreamers. The specialist is the šāʾilu or, most characteristically, the šāʾiltu — 'the woman who asks' — one of the few esoteric offices in Mesopotamia regularly held by women, who put questions to gods and to the dead as well as to dreams. Kings might also refer a dream to the bārû for confirmation by extispicy, one technique checking another. In the Sumerian tradition the interpreting power belongs to a goddess: in Gudea's Cylinder A the ruler of Lagaš, having dreamt of an enormous man crowned with a diadem, of a woman holding a stylus and a tablet of the starry sky, and of a donkey pawing the ground, carries the dream to Nanše at Nina, and she construes it item by item as the god Ningirsu's command to build his house. The Sumerian tale of Dumuzi's dream shows the same office in private hands: his sister Geštinanna reads his dream of scattered rushes and a fallen standard, and tells him that the demons are coming.","The practical problem was the bad dream, and here the exorcist rather than the interpreter took over. A dream that announced evil could be undone by a namburbû exactly as a bad omen could: the dreamer rose before sunrise and presented himself to Šamaš as the sun came up; he told the dream to a lump of clay which was then dissolved in water, or it was transferred to a reed or a figurine which was burnt or floated away downstream. The formula of these rites is one of substitution and dispatch — let the evil of this dream attach to this object, and let the object go where the river goes. Some of the same rites reverse a dream's sense outright, turning an ill dream into a good one by declaring it so at the moment of sunrise, when the sun-god's judgement was held to be handed down.","Deliberate dreaming — incubation — is attested but less common than in the Greek world, and is characteristically royal or priestly rather than popular. Gudea sleeps at the temple to obtain his building command. Assurbanipal's annals give the clearest Assyrian instance: on the eve of the campaign against Teumman of Elam the king prayed before Ištar of Arbela, and in the night a šabrû, an ecstatic, lay down and saw the goddess with her quiver at her side and her bow in her hand, who told him that she would go before Assurbanipal. A second episode in the same annals has the goddess send a dream to the army halted at the flooded Idideʾ, with the same promise, after which the crossing was made. A deity of dreams, Mamu, reckoned a child of Šamaš and appearing in the sources with more than one gender, had a temple at Imgur-Enlil, modern Balawat, built by Ashurnasirpal II. What the whole discipline assumes is that sleep is a place where a man is accessible: the god comes and stands at his head, and speaks, and the business of the specialist is to determine which god it was and what precisely was said."],terms:[{native:"𒈧𒈪",translit:"MÁŠ.GE₆ — šuttu",gloss:"'Dream', written with the MÁŠ sign (Unicode MASH2, U+12227) and GE₆ 'night' (the MI sign); the standard logogram in both omen and letter texts."},{native:"zaqīqu",translit:"zaqīqu / ziqīqu",gloss:"Breath, phantom, spirit — and the god of dreams who carries them; the series bears his name. Given in transliteration."},{native:"šāʾiltu",translit:"šāʾiltu",gloss:"'She who asks': the female dream-interpreter, who also questioned gods and ghosts. Given in transliteration."},{native:"šabrû",translit:"šabrû",gloss:"An ecstatic or dream-priest, the figure who receives a dream on another's behalf, as before Assurbanipal's campaign against Teumman. Given in transliteration."},{native:"𒉆𒁔𒁉",translit:"NAM.BÚR.BI — namburbû",gloss:"'Its undoing'; used of dreams as of omens, dissolving the announced evil into clay, water or a substitute object at sunrise."},{native:"Mamu",translit:"Mamu",gloss:"Deity of dreams, reckoned a child of Šamaš, whose temple at Imgur-Enlil (Balawat) was built by Ashurnasirpal II. Given in transliteration; the sources are not consistent about the deity's gender."}],texts:[{title:"Iškar Zaqīqu, the 'Dream Book'",note:"The dream-omen series, reckoned at eleven tablets though the sequence is only partly fixed; chiefly from Assurbanipal's libraries at Nineveh. Edited by A. L. Oppenheim, The Interpretation of Dreams in the Ancient Near East (1956)."},{title:"Gudea, Cylinder A",note:"From Girsu, c. 2100 BC: the ruler's dream of the temple-command, expounded to him clause by clause by the goddess Nanše — the fullest narrative of incubation and interpretation in Sumerian."},{title:"Dumuzi's Dream",note:"Sumerian composition in which Geštinanna interprets her brother's dream of scattered rushes and a fallen standard as the approach of the demons who will take him."},{title:"Namburbi rites against an evil dream",note:"Rituals for cancelling a dream at sunrise — the dream told to clay and dissolved in water, or transferred to a reed or figurine and dispatched downstream. Collected by S. Maul (1994) and treated by S. A. L. Butler (1998)."},{title:"Assurbanipal's annals: the dream before the Teumman campaign",note:"A šabrû's night vision of Ištar of Arbela, armed, promising to go before the king; a second passage has the goddess send a dream to the army halted at the Idideʾ."}]},{slug:"incantations",title:"Incantations",native:"𒅗𒅗𒈠",translit:"KA.INIM.MA — ka-inim-ma",summary:"The formal architecture of the spoken rite: how an incantation declares its use, and by whose authority the man reciting it speaks.",paragraphs:["A Mesopotamian incantation is a formally bounded object, and its boundaries are visible on the tablet. It opens with ÉN, the word šiptu, 'incantation' — in the oldest Sumerian material spelt out as the archaic formula én-é-nu-ru, whose meaning was already obscure to the scribes who copied it — and it closes with the rubric ka-inim-ma, 'the wording of', followed by a statement of exactly what the text is for: the wording for a headache, for a woman in difficult labour, for a scorpion, for the burying of a figurine. After the rubric comes a second rubric, 'its ritual', introducing the physical actions: the text names what was to be made, of what material, how many times the words were to be said over it, and what was then done with it. The tablet is thus at once a script and a set of stage directions, and it never presents the words as sufficient in themselves.","The most consequential formal device in the corpus is what Falkenstein named the Marduk-Ea type, and it exists to solve a problem of authority. A man cannot command a demon. So the incantation tells a story instead: the god Asalluḫi — in the Akkadian tradition Marduk — sees what is happening to the sufferer, and goes to his father in the apsû to report it; Ea answers that his son knows already whatever he himself knows, that there is nothing he can add to him, and then gives the remedy in full, instructing him to go and act. The exorcist recited this narrative, and in reciting it stepped into the place it describes. When he then performed the prescribed acts, the words were not his; he was the point at which Ea's wisdom, relayed through Marduk, reached the patient. Many incantations end by saying so outright — the incantation is not mine, it is the incantation of Ea and Marduk — and the disclaimer is the whole basis of the exorcist's legitimacy. He is not a magician exerting personal power. He is a conduit, and the conduit is licensed.","The language of that conduit was Sumerian long after Sumerian had ceased to be spoken. As a vernacular it was probably dead by the early second millennium, though how early is debated; as the language of incantation it continued for a further millennium and a half, learned in the scribal schools and copied at Nineveh and Babylon and Uruk down to the Hellenistic period. Much of the material was set out bilingually, a line of Sumerian with its Akkadian rendering beneath — the whole of Udug-ḫul is arranged so — and a scribe could thus recite in the ancient tongue while understanding what he said. Sumerian was not retained by inertia. It was the language in which the world's arrangements had first been spoken, and antiquity was itself a form of efficacy: an incantation was held to be more powerful for being old, and for being in the words in which Enki had originally given it.","Beyond the great demon-series lie the working genres. Muššuʾu, 'Rubbing', nine tablets, provides incantations recited while a patient was massaged limb by limb from head to foot. Asakkū marṣūtu, reckoned at twelve tablets, treats the fever-demon. The Akkadian šuillakku or 'hand-lifting' prayer, written ŠU.ÍL.LÁ, is the most personal genre of all — a formal address to a named god, with praise, complaint and petition, spoken by an individual before a censer at night, and it shades into the sort of prayer that any devout person might have felt as prayer rather than as technique. And beneath all of these lie the Old Babylonian collections from Nippur, Isin and elsewhere: short, vivid, unsystematised texts against scorpions, worms in the teeth, quarrelling neighbours and difficult births, out of which the canonical series were eventually assembled."],terms:[{native:"𒅗𒅗𒈠",translit:"KA.INIM.MA — ka-inim-ma",gloss:"'Wording of the mouth': the Sumerian rubric that closes an incantation and states its use. Written with the KA sign twice — once for ka 'mouth', once for inim 'word', both values of the same sign — followed by MA."},{native:"én-é-nu-ru",translit:"ÉN.É.NU.RU",gloss:"The archaic opening formula of Sumerian incantations, later abbreviated to ÉN alone. Given in transliteration: the Unicode cuneiform repertoire contains no sign named EN2, and the identification of the sign used for én is not one to assert here."},{native:"šiptu",translit:"šiptu (ÉN)",gloss:"'Incantation'; the Akkadian word behind the ÉN rubric that opens the text. Given in transliteration."},{native:"Asalluḫi",translit:"dASAL.LÚ.ḪI",gloss:"The Sumerian exorcist-god, son of Enki, identified with Marduk in the Akkadian tradition; the divine speaker of the Marduk-Ea formula. Given in transliteration."},{native:"𒋗𒅍𒇲",translit:"ŠU.ÍL.LÁ — šuillakku",gloss:"'Hand-lifting': a formal individual prayer to a named god, recited before a censer, with praise, complaint and petition. The third sign is LÁ, that is la₂, which is written with the LAL sign (U+121F2) and not with LA."},{native:"dùdùbi",translit:"DÙ.DÙ.BI — epuštašu",gloss:"'Its ritual': the rubric introducing the physical actions that accompany a recitation; also written KÌD.KÌD.BI. Given in transliteration."}],texts:[{title:"Udug-ḫul (Utukkū lemnūtu)",note:"Sixteen tablets, Sumerian with interlinear Akkadian; the principal vehicle of the Marduk-Ea legitimation formula. Edited by M. J. Geller (BAM 8, 2016)."},{title:"Muššuʾu, 'Rubbing'",note:"Nine tablets of incantations recited while the patient was massaged limb by limb from the head downward. Edited by B. Böck (2007)."},{title:"Asakkū marṣūtu",note:"Bilingual series against the asakku fever-demon, reckoned at twelve tablets, closely related in idiom to Udug-ḫul."},{title:"Šuila prayers",note:"The Akkadian 'hand-lifting' prayers to individual gods, studied by W. Mayer (1976); the most personal register in the exorcist's repertoire."},{title:"Old Babylonian incantation collections",note:"Tablets from Nippur, Isin and elsewhere, second millennium: short unsystematised texts against scorpions, toothworm, quarrels and difficult birth, the raw material of the later series."}]},{slug:"demonology",title:"Demonology",native:"𒋗𒄇𒈠",translit:"ŠU.GIDIM.MA — qāt eṭemmi",summary:"The population of the invisible, illness as assault, and the diagnostic handbook that told an exorcist whose hand had struck.",paragraphs:["The demons are not an underworld opposition to the gods; they are, for the most part, functionaries. The utukku, written UDUG, is a spirit that may be evil or protective; the gallû is a bailiff of the netherworld, sent to arrest and to fetch, as the gallû-demons fetch Dumuzi; the rābiṣu is the 'croucher', the lurker who waits in a doorway or at a roof-corner; the ālû settles over a man like a garment and takes his speech; the aḫḫāzu is 'the seizer'; Namtar is Fate itself, the vizier of Ereškigal, and the asakku a disease that fastens on the body. Alongside them stand the sexually restless dead — the lilû and lilītu, and the ardat lilî, the girl who died unmarried and returns to trouble the living. Even so domestic a place as the privy has its resident, Šulak, who strikes the man who enters unclean. And against them stand equally invisible protectors, the šēdu and the lamassu, the personal guardians a man may lose and whose loss is itself a diagnosis.","Two figures dominate the popular material. Lamaštu is a daughter of Anu, expelled from heaven for her nature, and she is not a demon at all in the strict sense but a goddess acting on her own authority: she takes infants from the breast and the unborn from the womb, and the amulets show her lion-headed, taloned, with snakes in her hands, suckling a piglet and a whelp, kneeling on a donkey which is being loaded into a boat — for the operative aim of the ritual was to give her provisions, put her aboard and send her downriver. The canonical Lamaštu series runs to three tablets. Her chief adversary is Pazuzu, who is himself a demon: son of Ḫanbu, king of the lilû-spirits, bringer of the west wind, dog-faced, with a scorpion's tail and raptor's talons. He is met most often as a head alone, cast in bronze and worn at the throat, or appearing over the top edge of a Lamaštu plaque, staring down at her from above the scene — a demon set to police a goddess, which is a precise expression of how the system worked.","Illness was assault, and the exorcist's first task was to identify the assailant. The idiom is 'the hand of' — qāt Šamaš, qāt Ištar, qāt Sîn, qāt eṭemmi, the hand of a god or a goddess or of a ghost, and qāt māmīti, the hand of a curse. ŠU.GIDIM.MA, the hand of a ghost, is among the commonest diagnoses in the corpus, and it carries a social meaning: a ghost afflicts because it is unburied, or unfed, or has no descendant to pour water for it, and the cure therefore involved settling accounts with the dead — offerings, the naming of the ghost, and in the more elaborate rites a figurine of it provided with travelling provisions and buried, or handed over to Šamaš for conveyance to the netherworld.","The instrument of diagnosis is Sakikkû, 'Sick Sinews', forty tablets in six chapters, and it is one of the most impressive intellectual documents of the ancient world. Its first tablet does not describe a patient at all: it lists what the exorcist saw on his way to the house — the animals that crossed his path, what he met at the door — because the approach was already legible. The subsequent chapters work down the body from the crown of the head to the feet, then treat fevers, the course of a disease day by day, and finally the diseases of women and of infants. Its editor was Esagil-kīn-apli, and the catalogue in which he describes his work speaks of the older copies as tangled threads for which no duplicate existed, which he set in order and fixed. Beside Sakikkû stand the therapeutic tablets and, distinct from the āšipu, the asû or physician with his poultices and decoctions; the two professions overlapped, corresponded and, in the Nineveh letters, competed."],terms:[{native:"𒋗𒄇𒈠",translit:"ŠU.GIDIM.MA — qāt eṭemmi",gloss:"'Hand of a ghost': the commonest aetiology in the diagnostic corpus. ŠU is the hand, GIDIM the ghost of the dead."},{native:"𒌜",translit:"UDUG — utukku",gloss:"A spirit, evil or protective according to context; the evil utukku gives its name to the sixteen-tablet bilingual series. Written with the sign the standard repertoire names UDUG (U+1231C)."},{native:"𒊓𒍼",translit:"SA.GIG — Sakikkû",gloss:"'Sick sinews': the Diagnostic Handbook, forty tablets in six chapters, edited into its canonical form by Esagil-kīn-apli."},{native:"dDÌM.ME",translit:"dDÌM.ME — Lamaštu",gloss:"Given in transliteration: the logogram is written with the sign DÌM, that is dim₃, and the Unicode cuneiform repertoire contains no character of that name — only DIM and DIM2 — so the writing is not one to assemble here. Daughter of Anu, taker of infants."},{native:"Pazuzu",translit:"Pazuzu",gloss:"Son of Ḫanbu, king of the lilû-demons, bringer of the west wind; worn as a bronze head against Lamaštu. Given in transliteration."},{native:"rābiṣu",translit:"rābiṣu",gloss:"'The croucher': the demon that waits in a doorway or at the corner of a roof. Given in transliteration."},{native:"ardat lilî",translit:"ardat lilî",gloss:"The girl dead before marriage, who returns restless to the living; paired with lilû and lilītu. Given in transliteration."}],texts:[{title:"Sakikkû (SA.GIG), the Diagnostic Handbook",note:"Forty tablets in six chapters; Tablet 1 reads the omens seen on the way to the patient's house, the rest proceed from head to foot and on to fevers, prognosis, women and infants. Editions by R. Labat (1951) and N. Heeßel (AOAT 43, 2000)."},{title:"The Lamaštu series",note:"Three tablets of incantations and rites for provisioning the goddess and sending her downriver, with a large amulet tradition. Edited by W. Farber (2014)."},{title:"Utukkū lemnūtu (Udug-ḫul)",note:"Sixteen bilingual tablets against the evil utukku and his kind; the exorcist's principal general armoury."},{title:"Pazuzu heads and plaques",note:"Bronze and stone amulets bearing a standard inscription in which the demon names himself son of Ḫanbu and king of the lilû-spirits. Studied by N. Heeßel (2002)."},{title:"Ghost rituals",note:"Rites for identifying, feeding, naming and dispatching an afflicting eṭemmu, including figurine burials and consignment to Šamaš. Collected by J. Scurlock (2006)."}]},{slug:"protective-figurines",title:"Protective Figurines",native:"𒉣𒈨",translit:"NUN.ME — apkallu / abgal",summary:"A ritual that prescribed burying guardians beneath a house, and the excavated boxes in which archaeologists found what it prescribes.",paragraphs:["Among the exorcist's rituals is one titled by its purpose: to block the entry of the foot of evil into a man's house — šēp lemutti ina bīt amēli parāsu. It is a text of instructions to a craftsman-priest. It specifies figurines to be made, of what material and in what number: sages in the cloak of a fish, sages with the head and wings of a bird, sages with a human face carrying the bucket and the purifier, the bull-man, the lion-headed ugallu, the scorpion-man, the mušḫuššu-serpent, and dogs. It specifies how they were to be consecrated, what was to be recited over them, and precisely where they were to be set — in brick boxes beneath the threshold of the outer gate, in the corners of the rooms, beneath the floor at the head of the master's bed, in sevens. Then it is silent, because from that point the guardians worked invisibly, and the house was a fortified position whose garrison lay buried under its own floors.","The remarkable thing is that the deposits exist. Woolley found brick boxes of small clay figures beneath house floors at Ur. Excavators at Assur and at Nimrud recovered comparable groups from Assyrian buildings, and at Nineveh sets of five clay dogs, each with a name inscribed along its flank — names of the sort that identify a temperament rather than an individual: loud of bark, biter of his foe, expeller of evil — were found where the ritual said dogs should be, the ritual itself prescribing dogs of differing colours. The most eloquent case is the house of an exorcist in Seleucid Uruk, where figurine deposits were recovered along with the family's library, so that the tablets prescribing the practice and the objects produced by it came out of the same building. It is a rare and valuable alignment: the archaeology confirms that these texts were not literary exercises but instructions that men in fact followed.","The figures at the head of the hierarchy are the apkallū, the sages, written NUN.ME and called abgal in Sumerian. They are antediluvian: seven of them, sent up out of the apsû by Ea before the flood to give mankind the arts of civilisation, and the third tablet of Bīt mēseri preserves their names — Uanna, whom the Greeks knew as Oannes and whom the Uruk list equates with Adapa; Uannedugga; Enmedugga; Enmegalamma; Enmebulugga; Anenlilda; and Utuabzu, who is said to have ascended to heaven. The first of them wore the skin of a carp over his head and back, and it is this fish-cloak that the reliefs and the figurines reproduce. After the flood the line continues in a lower key as the ummânū, the human scholars, and a Seleucid tablet from Uruk sets out the pairing formally, king by king with his sage, down to Esarhaddon, whose scholar it names as Aba-Enlil-dari, whom it says the Aramaeans call Aḫuqar — the Ahiqar of the later legend.","The operative logic is worth stating plainly, because it is not what a modern reader expects. The buried figurines were not symbols and not offerings. They were personnel. The rite created a standing guard of beings drawn from the oldest and most authoritative stratum of the world — the sages who were there before the flood, and dogs, which are the ordinary and effective guardians of an ordinary door — and stationed them at exactly the points a house is vulnerable: thresholds, corners, the sleeper's head. Evil in this system moves like a person. It walks, it has a foot, it comes to a door and looks for a way in. The remedy is therefore not exorcism but garrison duty, and it continued, silently, under the floor, for as long as the house stood."],terms:[{native:"𒉣𒈨",translit:"NUN.ME — apkallu / abgal",gloss:"The sage. Seven antediluvian apkallū were sent from the apsû by Ea to teach mankind; the writing NUN.ME is the Sumerian abgal."},{native:"šēp lemutti",translit:"šēp lemutti",gloss:"'The foot of evil', from the ritual title 'to block the entry of the foot of evil into a man's house'. Given in transliteration."},{native:"𒄊",translit:"GÌR — šēpu",gloss:"'Foot'; the logogram behind the ritual's title, and a reminder that evil in this literature walks and must be met at a threshold."},{native:"ūmu-apkallu",translit:"ūmu-apkallu",gloss:"In Wiggermann's classification, the human-faced winged sage who carries bucket and purifier; the bird-headed griffin-figure and the fish-cloaked sage are the other two apkallu types the ritual distinguishes. Given in transliteration."},{native:"banduddû",translit:"banduddû / mullilu",gloss:"The bucket and the purifier — cone or date-spathe — carried by the apkallu figures in relief and in clay. Given in transliteration."},{native:"ugallu",translit:"ugallu",gloss:"'Great weather-beast', the lion-headed guardian with a raised weapon, among the figurine types prescribed for burial. Given in transliteration."}],texts:[{title:"Šēp lemutti ina bīt amēli parāsu",note:"'To block the entry of the foot of evil into a man's house': the apotropaic figurine ritual, with manuscripts from Nineveh and Assur. Edited by F. A. M. Wiggermann, Mesopotamian Protective Spirits: The Ritual Texts (1992)."},{title:"Bīt mēseri, Tablet III",note:"Preserves the names of the seven antediluvian apkallū and the placing of their figurines around the enclosed patient."},{title:"The Nineveh dog figurines",note:"Clay dogs recovered in fives from the Assyrian palaces, each inscribed along the flank with a name describing its function, matching the ritual's prescription of buried dogs."},{title:"Figurine deposits at Ur",note:"Brick boxes of small clay figures set beneath the floors of private houses, excavated by Woolley in the domestic quarter."},{title:"The exorcist's house at Uruk",note:"A Seleucid dwelling in which figurine deposits and the family's ritual library were recovered together — text and practice from one building."},{title:"The Uruk List of Kings and Sages",note:"Seleucid tablet pairing each king with his apkallu or ummânu, naming Esarhaddon's scholar as Aba-Enlil-dari, called Aḫuqar by the Aramaeans."}]},{slug:"temple-ritual",title:"Temple Ritual",native:"𒀉𒆠𒋾",translit:"á-ki-ti — akītu",summary:"The god as a resident to be fed and clothed, the ceremony that turned a manufactured statue into a living god, and the New Year at Babylon.",paragraphs:["A Mesopotamian temple is a household, and its central fact is that the god lives there. The image in the cella is not a representation of the deity; after the proper ceremony it is the deity, present, embodied and requiring what a body requires. The Seleucid ritual tablets from Uruk preserve the routine in detail: the gate was opened at dawn with its own rite, water brought for the god's hands, and four meals served each day — a main and a second meal in the morning, a main and a second in the evening — with bread, beer, wine, milk, dates, meat and fruit set out on the table. Curtains were drawn about the table while the god ate, so that no one should see the meal taken. Afterwards the food, now charged by its contact, was carried out as rēḫāti, 'leftovers', to the king and the temple personnel: the god's dinner descended through the hierarchy as a blessing. Clothing was as carefully managed, the images dressed in garments of fixed weight and quality at fixed points in the calendar in the ceremony called lubuštu, and the great Neo-Babylonian temple archives are full of the administration of this — the gold, the wool, the dates, the sheep, the accounting of a divine domestic economy.","The transition from carpentry to divinity was made by mīs pî and pīt pî, the Washing and the Opening of the Mouth. The statue was made in the temple workshop, the bīt mummu, of a wooden core plated with gold and inlaid with stones. It was then taken out over two days and two nights: to the riverbank, into a reed hut in an orchard, set facing east and then west, seated on reed matting, with offerings to the gods of the night and to the divine craftsmen. Its mouth was washed repeatedly with tamarisk, soapwort and holy water, and opened with the appropriate incantations, and only then could it eat, smell incense and drink; a statue whose mouth had not been opened, the texts say plainly, cannot do these things. Most striking is the disavowal. The human craftsmen who made it were brought forward, their hands symbolically severed with a sword of tamarisk wood, and made to swear that they had not made it — that it was made by Ninagal, Kusibanda, Ninildu, Ninkurra and Ninzadim, the gods respectively of the smith, the goldsmith, the carpenter, the stoneworker and the lapidary. The incantations declare the image born in heaven. The rite thus contains, and formally annuls, the objection to which the whole practice is exposed.","At Babylon the year turned on the akītu, reckoned at eleven or twelve days of Nisannu, and the surviving manuscripts — Seleucid copies of a text of uncertain age — cover chiefly the second to the fifth days. On the fourth day, in the evening, the šešgallu, the high priest, recited Enūma Eliš through from beginning to end before Marduk. What that recitation was understood to accomplish the tablet does not say; the once-standard reading, that the creation was annually re-enacted and the cosmos thereby renewed, belongs to a 'ritual pattern' school of interpretation that specialists have largely abandoned, and it should not be repeated as though the text asserted it. On the fifth the temple was purified, the chapel of Nabû wiped down with the carcass of a sheep, which was thrown with its head into the river; the exorcist and the slaughterer who did it were thereby unclean and had to leave the city until the festival was over.","Then came the king. The ritual text describes his being brought before Marduk; the high priest took from him his sceptre, his ring, his mace and his crown, and laid them before the god. He struck the king on the cheek, pulled his ears, and made him kneel, and the king made a declaration in the negative: that he had not sinned against Babylon, had not neglected the god's divinity, had not destroyed the walls or humiliated the citizens or failed to maintain the temple. The insignia were restored, the priest struck him on the cheek a second time, and an omen was read from that blow — if the king's tears came, Bēl was favourable; if not, the god was angry. This is one of the most quoted passages in Assyriology and one of the least securely grounded: it survives in Hellenistic manuscripts, in a damaged text, and no source records any particular king undergoing it, so how old the ceremony is and how often it was actually performed are open questions. The days that follow are known from other sources rather than from the preserved tablets: Nabû arrived from Borsippa, destinies were determined in the chamber of assembly, and the gods went in procession through the Ištar Gate and along the Processional Way to the akītu house outside the city."],terms:[{native:"𒀉𒆠𒋾",translit:"á-ki-ti — akītu",gloss:"The New Year festival, written á-ki-ti in Sumerian with the signs Á, KI and TI; Akkadian akītu, and the name also of the festival house outside the city."},{native:"mīs pî",translit:"mīs pî (KA.LUḪ.Ù.DA)",gloss:"'Washing of the mouth'. Given in transliteration: the Unicode repertoire has KA, LUḪ and DA but no sign named U3, so the Sumerian writing cannot be assembled here with confidence."},{native:"pīt pî",translit:"pīt pî (KA.DUḪ.Ù.DA)",gloss:"'Opening of the mouth', the paired rite that gives the image the capacity to eat, smell and drink. Given in transliteration, for the same reason."},{native:"šešgallu",translit:"šešgallu",gloss:"'Great brother': the high priest of Esagil who recites Enūma Eliš and, in the ritual text, administers the king's humiliation. Given in transliteration."},{native:"naptanu",translit:"naptanu",gloss:"The god's meal; four were served daily, main and second, morning and evening. Given in transliteration."},{native:"rēḫāti",translit:"rēḫāti",gloss:"'Leftovers': the god's food after he had eaten, redistributed to the king and temple staff as a conveyed blessing. Given in transliteration."},{native:"bīt mummu",translit:"bīt mummu",gloss:"The temple workshop in which cult images were made and where the mouth-washing sequence began. Given in transliteration."}],texts:[{title:"The mīs pî / pīt pî ritual",note:"Ritual tablets in Nineveh and Babylon versions with a large body of accompanying incantations, prescribing two days and nights of washing, orchard, riverbank and the craftsmen's disavowal. Edited by C. Walker and M. Dick (SAALT 1, 2001)."},{title:"The Babylon akītu ritual",note:"Seleucid manuscripts, damaged, covering chiefly days two to five of Nisannu: the recitation of Enūma Eliš, the purification of the temple, and the king's humiliation before Bēl. Published by F. Thureau-Dangin, Rituels accadiens (1921); re-edited in M. Linssen (2004)."},{title:"Enūma Eliš",note:"Seven tablets, recited in full by the šešgallu on the fourth evening of the festival, for a purpose the ritual text does not specify."},{title:"The Uruk daily offering text",note:"Seleucid tablet setting out the god's four daily meals, the opening of the gate, the water for the hands and the disposal of the leftovers. Studied in M. Linssen, The Cults of Uruk and Babylon (2004)."},{title:"The 'Marduk Ordeal'",note:"Assyrian commentary-text reinterpreting the Babylonian festival as the trial and imprisonment of Marduk; usually read as polemic against a rival city's liturgy, though the purpose has been disputed (SAA 3)."},{title:"Eanna and Ebabbar temple archives",note:"Neo-Babylonian administrative records of the divine household at Uruk and Sippar: garments and their weights for the lubuštu, offerings, herds and the officials responsible."}]},{slug:"lamentation-purification",title:"Lamentation and Purification",native:"𒇽𒍑𒆪",translit:"LÚ.UŠ.KU (GALA) — kalû; the craft, kalûtu",summary:"The profession of ritual weeping, the dialect it wept in, and the technologies of transfer by which impurity was moved out of a body.",paragraphs:["The kalû — written with the GALA sign, that is UŠ.KU, under the LÚ determinative — is the singer of laments, and his craft, kalûtu, is a technical discipline with its own corpus, its own instruments and its own dialect. That dialect is Emesal, written EME.SAL, 'the thin tongue', a variant register of Sumerian used for the speech of goddesses in literature and, in ritual, for the whole of the kalû's repertoire. Its two central genres are the balaĝ, named for the instrument that accompanied it, a long composition addressed to an angry god and cataloguing the desolation of his abandoned city; and the eršemma, 'the wail of the šem-drum', shorter and often appended to a balaĝ as its conclusion. To these is added the eršaḫuĝa, the lament for appeasing the heart, addressed to a personal god who has turned away. Late ritual calendars from Uruk and Babylon prescribe which balaĝ was to be sung on which day, which shows that this was liturgy in the strict sense, fixed, dated and repeated.","Why weeping should be an instrument is explained by the tradition itself. In Inanna's Descent, when the goddess hangs dead on a hook in the netherworld, Enki makes two creatures from the dirt under his fingernails and sends them down with instructions: when Ereškigal groans over her body, they are to groan with her, echoing her cries word for word. She is soothed — not persuaded, soothed — and in gratitude gives them what they ask. That is the operative model. Lament does not argue with a god; it matches his mood until the heart, the libbu, is quieted, and only then can anything be asked. The great Sumerian city laments — the Lament for Ur, in which Ningal pleads through the night before An and Enlil for her city and is refused, and the Lamentation over the Destruction of Sumer and Ur, with its storm sent as a decision of the divine assembly — were composed in the Isin period, and the evidence suggests they served the rebuilding of the very temples whose destruction they describe. The kalû sang at the demolition of a ruined wall before it was rebuilt, so that the god should not take the disturbance amiss.","The kalû also owned the bronze kettledrum, the lilissu, and its manufacture was itself a ritual and an exact parallel to the making of a divine statue. A black bull without blemish was chosen and led into the workshop; the kalû recited into its ear; it was slaughtered, its heart burnt, its sinews and hide prepared, and the hide stretched over the bronze bowl. Here too the human agency was disavowed and the work assigned to the gods, and here too the disavowal was necessary precisely because everyone present knew who had actually held the knife. Ea, under the name Lumḫa, was claimed as the divine patron of the craft.","Purification proper worked by transfer, and its instruments were water, fire and boundary. The holy-water vessel, the egubbû, was compounded with tamarisk, soapwort, salt and other purifying materials; the censer and the torch — nignakku and gizillû — were carried around the sufferer so that smoke and flame passed over him; a circle of flour, the zisurrû, was drawn to fix a boundary the invisible could not cross. And where the evil could not be destroyed, it was moved. A substitute was provided: the pūḫu or dinānu, most often a kid, set to stand in the man's place, its head for his head, its neck for his neck, its breast for his breast; or the mašḫultuppû, the goat driven out bearing the trouble away; or a figurine of clay or dough or wax, made to receive the affliction and then buried in the steppe, floated downriver, or burnt. The underlying conviction is consistent across the whole corpus. Evil is a substance and a quantity. It could not be wished away, but it could be got to go somewhere else, and the whole apparatus of the exorcist and the lamentation priest is a technology for arranging its departure."],terms:[{native:"𒇽𒍑𒆪",translit:"lúGALA (UŠ.KU) — kalû",gloss:"The lamentation priest; GALA is a compound written UŠ.KU, here under the LÚ determinative. His craft, kalûtu, is one of the great scholarly disciplines."},{native:"𒅴𒊩",translit:"EME.SAL — Emesal",gloss:"'The thin tongue': the Sumerian register used for the speech of goddesses and for the whole of the kalû's liturgy. EME is the KA sign with ME inscribed; SAL is the sign for woman."},{native:"𒁆",translit:"BALAĜ — balaĝ",gloss:"The instrument, probably a drum, and the long Emesal lament named after it, addressed to an angry god over the ruin of his city."},{native:"eršemma",translit:"eršemma",gloss:"'Wail of the šem-drum': the shorter lament, frequently appended to a balaĝ as its conclusion. Given in transliteration."},{native:"eršaḫuĝa",translit:"eršaḫuĝa",gloss:"'Lament for appeasing the heart', addressed to a personal god who has turned his face away. Given in transliteration."},{native:"lilissu",translit:"lilissu",gloss:"The bronze kettledrum of the kalû, covered with the hide of a black bull in a ritual that disclaims human manufacture. Given in transliteration."},{native:"pūḫu",translit:"pūḫu / dinānu",gloss:"'Substitute': the kid, goat or figurine that takes a sufferer's place, member for member. Given in transliteration."},{native:"gizillû",translit:"gizillû / nignakku",gloss:"Torch and censer, carried around the patient so that flame and smoke pass over him. Given in transliteration."}],texts:[{title:"The Lament for Ur",note:"Sumerian city lament of the Isin period: Ningal's night-long plea before An and Enlil, refused, and the destruction of her city under the storm."},{title:"Lamentation over the Destruction of Sumer and Ur",note:"The longest of the city laments, presenting the fall as a decision taken in the divine assembly and executed as weather."},{title:"The balaĝ corpus",note:"Some thirty long Emesal compositions, transmitted from the early second millennium to the Hellenistic period and assigned to fixed days in the late ritual calendars. Edited by M. Cohen (1988)."},{title:"The eršemma corpus",note:"Shorter Emesal laments, often appended to balaĝ compositions; edited by M. Cohen (1981)."},{title:"The lilissu kettledrum ritual",note:"Seleucid copies from Uruk describing the choice of the black bull, the recitation into its ear, the treatment of the hide and the disavowal of human manufacture."},{title:"Substitution rites",note:"Rituals in which a kid, goat or figurine receives the evil member by member and is then buried, burnt or set adrift; many are collected within the namburbi corpus by S. Maul (1994)."}]},{slug:"mythic-cosmology",title:"Mythic Cosmology",native:"𒍪𒀊",translit:"ZU.AB — abzu / apsû",summary:"The shape of the world as the scholars taught it: a corpse made into a sky, a freshwater deep beneath the ground, and a mountain that is also the country of the dead.",paragraphs:["Enūma Eliš, seven tablets and something over a thousand lines, is the most consequential cosmological text of the ancient Near East, and its argument is political as much as cosmic. In the beginning are only Apsû, the sweet water, and Tiāmat, the sea, mingled; the younger gods are born, and are noisy, and Apsû resolves to destroy them until Ea puts him to sleep with an incantation and kills him, building his own dwelling upon him. Tiāmat, roused at last, produces eleven monsters and gives the tablet of destinies to Qingu. The gods can find no champion until Marduk agrees, on the condition that his word be made supreme. He takes her in his net, drives the winds into her open mouth, shoots the arrow that splits her heart, and then splits her body in two like a drying fish, setting one half above as the sky with a bolt and a guard to keep the waters from escaping. From her parts the world is furnished — her breasts made mountains, the Tigris and Euphrates set flowing from her eyes — and from the blood of Qingu, executed for the war, Ea makes man, so that the gods may be relieved of their labour. Babylon and Esagil are then built by the grateful Anunnaki, and the poem closes with the recitation of Marduk's fifty names. Assyrian copies replace him with Aššur throughout, which tells one exactly what the text was for.","Beneath the earth lies the apsû, written ZU.AB and read abzu: the freshwater ocean under the ground, source of springs and wells, dwelling of Ea, and the place from which wisdom comes. It is not the netherworld. The dead do not go there; the sages come from there, and so do incantations, which are Ea's property. Every temple had a basin called apsû standing in its courtyard, so the cosmic geography was physically present in the cult, and Eridu, the oldest city, was held to be built over it.","The technically esoteric account of the upper world survives in a small group of explanatory texts, of which the best known is a tablet from Assur usually cited as KAR 307, with a Babylonian counterpart. It gives three heavens and three earths. The uppermost heaven is of luludānītu stone and belongs to Anu, who settled three hundred Igigi in it; the middle heaven is of saggilmud stone, of the Igigi, where Bēl sits in a cella on a dais of lapis lazuli; the lowest heaven is of jasper, and the constellations of the gods are drawn upon it — which is to say the visible night sky is the underside of the lowest of three floors. Below, the upper earth is assigned to the souls of mankind, the middle earth is Ea's apsû, and the lowest is where the six hundred Anunnaki are confined. This is not popular cosmology and should not be taken as what an ordinary Babylonian pictured; it is scholarly exegesis, of the kind the second section of the Exorcist's Manual points towards, and it is preserved in very few witnesses.","The land of the dead is kur, and the word carries several senses at once — mountain, land, foreign country, netherworld — so that to go abroad, to go up into the hills and to die are held together in a single sign. Its fullest name is kur-nu-gi₄-a, the land of no return, and the descriptions are consistent and bleak: a house whose enterers do not come out, where dust lies on the door and the bolt, where the dead are clothed like birds in feathers and eat clay. Ereškigal rules it; there are seven gates, and at each the descending Inanna is stripped of one ornament until she arrives with nothing. The gods themselves are subject to it. Inanna returns only by providing a substitute, and the substitute is Dumuzi her husband, taken by the gallû-demons in his sheepfold; his sister Geštinanna offers herself, and the settlement reached is that each shall spend half the year below. Modern readers have often taken that alternation for a figure of the agricultural year, but the texts do not say so, and the wider 'dying and rising god' framework it belongs to has been heavily criticised. What is documented is the mourning: Neo-Assyrian calendars record the taklimtu, the lying-in-state of Dumuzi with wailing, in the fourth month, which still bore his name — as it did centuries later, when Ezekiel saw women at the north gate of the Temple weeping for Tammuz. And the world's arrangements themselves are the me, the divine offices, which Inanna obtains from Enki at Eridu by getting him drunk and loading them into the boat of heaven for Uruk; the lists that name them mix priestly offices, kingship, the crafts of the smith and the scribe and the leatherworker, the kindling of fire, the descent into the netherworld, sexual life, strife and the plundering of cities — civilisation entire, with nothing edifying removed."],terms:[{native:"𒍪𒀊",translit:"ZU.AB — abzu / apsû",gloss:"The freshwater deep beneath the earth, dwelling of Ea and source of wisdom and incantations; written with the signs ZU and AB but read abzu."},{native:"𒈨",translit:"ME — me / parṣu",gloss:"The divine offices or ordinances that constitute civilisation; taken by Inanna from Enki at Eridu and carried to Uruk. Akkadian parṣu."},{native:"𒆳",translit:"KUR — kur / mātu / šadû / erṣetu",gloss:"Mountain, land, foreign country and netherworld in one sign — a single written form holding together height, foreignness and death."},{native:"𒆳𒉡𒄄𒀀",translit:"KUR.NU.GI₄.A — erṣet lā târi",gloss:"'The land of no return': the netherworld, written KUR ('land'), NU ('not'), GI₄ ('return'), A."},{native:"𒀭𒂗𒆠",translit:"dEN.KI — Enki",gloss:"'Lord of the earth', god of the apsû, of wisdom, incantation and craft; called Ea in Akkadian, written dÉ.A (𒀭𒂍𒀀)."},{native:"𒀭𒀫𒌓",translit:"dAMAR.UTU — Marduk",gloss:"'Bull-calf of the sun': the god of Babylon, made king of the gods in Enūma Eliš and given fifty names in its last two tablets. UTU is a value of the UD sign."},{native:"𒀭𒌉𒍣",translit:"dDUMU.ZI — Dumuzi",gloss:"'True son', the shepherd taken as Inanna's substitute in the netherworld; the TUR sign here has the value dumu, 'son'."},{native:"Tiāmat",translit:"Tiāmat",gloss:"The sea, the primeval mother whose divided body becomes the sky and the furniture of the world. Given in transliteration."}],texts:[{title:"Enūma Eliš",note:"Seven tablets, over a thousand lines: the defeat of Tiāmat, the making of the world from her body, the creation of man from Qingu's blood and the fifty names of Marduk. Edited by W. G. Lambert, Babylonian Creation Myths (2013)."},{title:"KAR 307 and its Babylonian counterpart",note:"Explanatory tablets setting out three heavens of luludānītu, saggilmud and jasper, and three earths — mankind's souls, the apsû, the Anunnaki. Edited by A. Livingstone (1986); discussed in W. Horowitz, Mesopotamian Cosmic Geography (1998)."},{title:"Inanna and Enki",note:"Sumerian narrative of the transfer of the me from Eridu to Uruk, with long lists naming the offices carried off in the boat of heaven."},{title:"Inanna's Descent to the Netherworld",note:"The seven gates, the stripping of the goddess, her death and revival, and the taking of Dumuzi as substitute; with an Akkadian counterpart, Ištar's Descent."},{title:"Atra-ḫasīs",note:"Three tablets, Old Babylonian, the manuscript written by the junior scribe Ku-Aya in the reign of Ammi-ṣaduqa: the labour of the gods, the making of man to bear it, and the flood sent against human noise."},{title:"Dumuzi in the ritual calendars",note:"Neo-Assyrian records of the taklimtu, the lying-in-state of Dumuzi with lamentation, in the fourth month, which bore his name."}]}]};export{e as default};
